    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\n\nA queue decouples producers and consumers and provides ordering/backpressure semantics.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nState the rule in one sentence, trace a small example from input to output, and identify the important edge cases.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\nasync function runTasks(\n  tasks: readonly (() => Promise<number>)[],\n  limit: number,\n): Promise<number[]> {\n  const result: number[] = [];\n  let next = 0;\n\n  async function worker(): Promise<void> {\n    while (next < tasks.length) {\n      const index = next;\n      next += 1;\n      result[index] = await tasks[index]();\n    }\n  }\n\n  const workers: Promise<void>[] = [];\n  for (\n    let i = 0;\n    i < Math.min(limit, tasks.length);\n    i += 1\n  ) {\n    workers.push(worker());\n  }\n\n  await Promise.all(workers);\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\n```ts\nasync function runTasks(\n  tasks: readonly (() => Promise<number>)[],\n): Promise<number[]> {\n  return Promise.all(\n    tasks.map((task) => task()),\n  );\n}\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse **Task Queue** only where it solves a real production requirement; define ownership, cleanup, failure handling, and observability explicitly.\n\nStep 6 — Edge cases:\nCheck empty/nullish inputs, repeated calls, boundaries, failure, cleanup, memory retention, concurrency, and browser/Node differences where relevant.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the semantic rule first, then implementation, complexity, failure mode, and why the production approach is appropriate.",
